R$ and $(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in R,$ and also $(\\beta ,\\beta )\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's not reflexive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e39d6c66-19f1-5b1f-bd77-f03b05414e51"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e39d6c66-19f1-5b1f-bd77-f03b05414e51" :answer-field/id #uuid "e39d6c66-19f1-5b1f-bd77-f03b05414e51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2945124f-634b-58b9-9263-8fb5d84a8223" :answer-field/choices ["answer-529bac0f-0ab2-5dd2-829d-897c7a169213" "answer-e584f397-eac7-55f4-9aac-875dd305d2ae" "answer-71db4ad8-a7c1-5de6-a688-cac7a9af590c" "answer-2945124f-634b-58b9-9263-8fb5d84a8223" "answer-b2d02066-46a0-505f-9c99-5c4bf970eed7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-529bac0f-0ab2-5dd2-829d-897c7a169213" :answer/id #uuid "529bac0f-0ab2-5dd2-829d-897c7a169213" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e584f397-eac7-55f4-9aac-875dd305d2ae" :answer/id #uuid "e584f397-eac7-55f4-9aac-875dd305d2ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71db4ad8-a7c1-5de6-a688-cac7a9af590c" :answer/id #uuid "71db4ad8-a7c1-5de6-a688-cac7a9af590c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2945124f-634b-58b9-9263-8fb5d84a8223" :answer/id #uuid "2945124f-634b-58b9-9263-8fb5d84a8223" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2d02066-46a0-505f-9c99-5c4bf970eed7" :answer/id #uuid "b2d02066-46a0-505f-9c99-5c4bf970eed7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a3f6ced1-e3e7-51b2-bdb3-c0561413aee0" :question/id #uuid "a3f6ced1-e3e7-51b2-bdb3-c0561413aee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-214246" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $N?$\n\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$\n- $a∣b$\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $3∣(a+b)$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is an equivalence relation. \n\n- It is reflexive because for all $a\\in N,$ we have that $a-a=0$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $a,b\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2,$ then $b-a$ is also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is transitive because for all $a,b,c\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$ and $b-c$ is a multiple of $2,$ then\n\n$$\na-c=(a-b)+(b-c)\n$$\n\nis also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $3∣6$ but $6∤3.$\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $3∤(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-36c1e14d-f406-53c3-919e-0c1fd156cc01"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-36c1e14d-f406-53c3-919e-0c1fd156cc01" :answer-field/id #uuid "36c1e14d-f406-53c3-919e-0c1fd156cc01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-914a9fe7-3947-5a9f-92ed-734d047aebca" "answer-b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" "answer-2224d9bb-0faf-5fc9-b610-28aaddc41507" "answer-ce69759e-4547-5b71-b4e3-eb3dc5f1b96e" "answer-cd6d8781-5676-564f-b9b9-dac7cffccc49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-914a9fe7-3947-5a9f-92ed-734d047aebca" :answer/id #uuid "914a9fe7-3947-5a9f-92ed-734d047aebca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" :answer/id #uuid "b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2224d9bb-0faf-5fc9-b610-28aaddc41507" :answer/id #uuid "2224d9bb-0faf-5fc9-b610-28aaddc41507" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce69759e-4547-5b71-b4e3-eb3dc5f1b96e" :answer/id #uuid "ce69759e-4547-5b71-b4e3-eb3dc5f1b96e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd6d8781-5676-564f-b9b9-dac7cffccc49" :answer/id #uuid "cd6d8781-5676-564f-b9b9-dac7cffccc49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c8d639d-4ba9-5766-b837-cc9cfbce29a2" :question/id #uuid "5c8d639d-4ba9-5766-b837-cc9cfbce29a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-214245" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A⊃B$\n- $A=B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}\\cap {1}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Relation II is also not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}⊅{1}.$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f758d976-bcdb-5e5f-af93-d6835069d66d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f758d976-bcdb-5e5f-af93-d6835069d66d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f758d976-bcdb-5e5f-af93-d6835069d66d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17261494-8ee1-5a6c-a36e-65e458355a8c" "answer-040a02e2-1bb9-514c-903f-81f6a2d7ea14" "answer-e8c7b953-d54f-55fc-9672-1c5535f4bd2d" "answer-18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" "answer-62992426-d8cb-559d-b581-acc3f07854b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17261494-8ee1-5a6c-a36e-65e458355a8c" :answer/id #uuid "17261494-8ee1-5a6c-a36e-65e458355a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-040a02e2-1bb9-514c-903f-81f6a2d7ea14" :answer/id #uuid "040a02e2-1bb9-514c-903f-81f6a2d7ea14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8c7b953-d54f-55fc-9672-1c5535f4bd2d" :answer/id #uuid "e8c7b953-d54f-55fc-9672-1c5535f4bd2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" :answer/id #uuid "18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62992426-d8cb-559d-b581-acc3f07854b5" :answer/id #uuid "62992426-d8cb-559d-b581-acc3f07854b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1a65003f-5b78-57dd-8ad6-282231fb787b" :question/id #uuid "1a65003f-5b78-57dd-8ad6-282231fb787b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76164" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $R?$\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy>0$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x=2y$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric because $2=2(1)$ but $1\\ne 2(2).$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation:\n\n- It is reflexive because for all $x\\in R,$ we have $x-x=0$ is a rational number.\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $x,y\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ is a rational number, then $y-x=-(x-y)=-r$ is also a rational number.\n\n- It is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ and $y-z=q,$ where $r,q$ are rational numbers, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)=r+q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da731a85-b255-5017-87f3-60d7f37421d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da731a85-b255-5017-87f3-60d7f37421d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "da731a85-b255-5017-87f3-60d7f37421d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d79a90aa-2e9b-5f04-b081-64d7cc3f75e4" "answer-589ce36f-fb09-55ca-ab3b-7f3aa6166186" "answer-661dd547-fab0-5221-9f01-e96464c7b3af" "answer-cf8cf821-8c18-5dd6-be5b-f58b8b837325" "answer-a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d79a90aa-2e9b-5f04-b081-64d7cc3f75e4" :answer/id #uuid "d79a90aa-2e9b-5f04-b081-64d7cc3f75e4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-589ce36f-fb09-55ca-ab3b-7f3aa6166186" :answer/id #uuid "589ce36f-fb09-55ca-ab3b-7f3aa6166186" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-661dd547-fab0-5221-9f01-e96464c7b3af" :answer/id #uuid "661dd547-fab0-5221-9f01-e96464c7b3af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf8cf821-8c18-5dd6-be5b-f58b8b837325" :answer/id #uuid "cf8cf821-8c18-5dd6-be5b-f58b8b837325" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651" :answer/id #uuid "a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0c07a7ee-9fb6-5236-b864-38ec61e9b0cc" :question/id #uuid "0c07a7ee-9fb6-5236-b864-38ec61e9b0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-214241" :question/problem "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A⊄B$\n- $|A|\\ne |B|$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- The relation \"$⊄$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since ${2,3}⊄{2}$ but ${2}⊂{2,3}.$\n\n- The relation \"$\\ne $\" for the cardinalities of sets is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $|{2}|=|{2}|.$\n\n- The relation \"$⊇$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $N⊇{1}$ but ${1}⊉N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-763d9cb9-42d8-5b2a-baa3-fc94d026c898"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-763d9cb9-42d8-5b2a-baa3-fc94d026c898" :answer-field/id #uuid "763d9cb9-42d8-5b2a-baa3-fc94d026c898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90760cd8-7871-5634-b9b5-3531a983c1aa" "answer-a6b3f0ee-7ced-5feb-9405-387aa70e3545" "answer-69c8f651-7fad-58ab-9225-eb16c24dc65f" "answer-fec83f8f-fd76-54ba-94a1-1cd6f83935d5" "answer-dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90760cd8-7871-5634-b9b5-3531a983c1aa" :answer/id #uuid "90760cd8-7871-5634-b9b5-3531a983c1aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6b3f0ee-7ced-5feb-9405-387aa70e3545" :answer/id #uuid "a6b3f0ee-7ced-5feb-9405-387aa70e3545" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69c8f651-7fad-58ab-9225-eb16c24dc65f" :answer/id #uuid "69c8f651-7fad-58ab-9225-eb16c24dc65f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fec83f8f-fd76-54ba-94a1-1cd6f83935d5" :answer/id #uuid "fec83f8f-fd76-54ba-94a1-1cd6f83935d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/id #uuid "dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-78aa39d2-6d95-5a7b-bd86-1b4658d3d903" :question/id #uuid "78aa39d2-6d95-5a7b-bd86-1b4658d3d903" :question/math-academy-id "q-214247" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $Q?$\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$\n- $x=y$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy\\in N$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${2}^{2}+{2}^{2}≰4.$\n\n- Relation II is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\notin N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-376584a2-7252-545c-be81-7372124fc83e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-376584a2-7252-545c-be81-7372124fc83e" :answer-field/id #uuid "376584a2-7252-545c-be81-7372124fc83e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4af48504-6f1c-5b90-87ab-46f546fb2eb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d179e1fc-abb6-5aca-92d5-246063f7fbc2" "answer-8a83063d-29fe-5569-893d-885aaf2bcb4c" "answer-6d984c53-eae2-5d52-bffe-bead8d751ef7" "answer-4af48504-6f1c-5b90-87ab-46f546fb2eb6" "answer-810ae78b-09ba-5372-b677-ed900baea3aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d179e1fc-abb6-5aca-92d5-246063f7fbc2" :answer/id #uuid "d179e1fc-abb6-5aca-92d5-246063f7fbc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a83063d-29fe-5569-893d-885aaf2bcb4c" :answer/id #uuid "8a83063d-29fe-5569-893d-885aaf2bcb4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d984c53-eae2-5d52-bffe-bead8d751ef7" :answer/id #uuid "6d984c53-eae2-5d52-bffe-bead8d751ef7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4af48504-6f1c-5b90-87ab-46f546fb2eb6" :answer/id #uuid "4af48504-6f1c-5b90-87ab-46f546fb2eb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-810ae78b-09ba-5372-b677-ed900baea3aa" :answer/id #uuid "810ae78b-09ba-5372-b677-ed900baea3aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153455 :tutorial/content "You may have noticed that the congruence of integers modulo $n$ satisfies the following important properties:\n\n- The *reflexive* property. Every integer is congruent to itself: \n\n$$\na \\equiv a \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\n- The *symmetric* property. If $a$ is congruent to $b,$ then $b$ is congruent to $a{:}$ \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b \\equiv a \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\n- The *transitive* property. If $a$ is congruent to $b,$ and $b$ is congruent to $c,$ then $a$ is congruent to $c{:}$ \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad b \\equiv c \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a \\equiv c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nGenerally, if a relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it's called an **equivalence relation**. The congruence of integers modulo $n$ is an example of an equivalence relation.\n\nThe symbol \"$\\sim$\" is often used to represent an equivalence relation. So, if $R$ is an equivalence relation, instead of $(x,y) \\in R$ or $x \\: R \\: y,$ one can simply write $x \\sim y.$\n\nSome other important equivalence relations include the following:\n\n- Equality ($=$) over $\\mathbb R$\n\n- Parity over $\\mathbb Z$\n\n- Triangle similarity over the (infinite) set containing all possible triangles\n\nIn particular, you may recall that when two triangles $P$ and $Q$ are similar, we write $P\\sim Q.$ Now you know why we use this notation!\n\nEquivalence relations are important because they encode our natural intuitions (namely, reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity) describing what it means for two objects to be, in some sense, equal, equivalent, or \"the same.\"\n\nTo understand this concretely, suppose we have a bag containing many colored balls, and we sort them into groups according to their color. Then, the following properties must be true:\n\n- Each ball is the same color as itself. Therefore, each ball satisfies the reflexive property.\n\n- If ball $x$ is the same color as ball $y,$ then ball $y$ is the same color as ball $x.$ Therefore, the balls within a given group satisfy the symmetric property.\n\n- If ball $x$ is the same color as ball $y,$ and ball $y$ is the same color as ball $z,$ then ball $x$ must be the same color as ball $z.$ Therefore, the balls within a given group satisfy the transitive property.\n\nSince the relation \"is the same color as\" over the set of balls is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it's an equivalence relation." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152329 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
